    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "\r\nThis change makes it difficult for plugins to gracefully replace the `-` mapping because it uses an anonymous function. Plugins like [vim-dirvish](https://github.com/justinmk/vim-dirvish/) often use this pattern to avoid clobbering user mappings:\r\n\r\n```vim\r\nif mapcheck('-', 'n') ==# '' && !hasmapto('<Plug>(dirvish_up)', 'n')\r\n  nmap - <Plug>(dirvish_up)\r\nendif\r\n```\r\n\r\nmapcheck gives an unhelpful function ref (I assume this means it's the 10th function defined, so not a stable value):\r\n\r\n```\r\n:Verbose echo mapcheck('-', 'n')\r\n<Lua 10>\r\n```\r\n\r\ndefaults are processed before user configuration so `let g:loaded_nvim_dir_plugin = 0` in my config won't fix.\r\n\r\nHowever, using the raw plug mapping gives something that file navigation plugins can check for and ignore:\r\n\r\n```vim\r\n:lua vim.keymap.set('n', '-', '<Plug>(nvim-dir-up)')\r\n:Verbose echo mapcheck('-', 'n')\r\n<Plug>(nvim-dir-up)\r\n```\r\n\r\n> also fall back to the builtin - motion if the dir.lua is disabled, preventing mappings being made to undefined things\r\n\r\nCould we map nvim-dir-up to - in dir.lua in the disabled case instead?\r\n```lua\r\nif vim.g.loaded_nvim_dir_plugin ~= nil then\r\n  vim.keymap.set('n', '<Plug>(nvim-dir-up)', '-', { silent = true, desc = 'Go up one line' })\r\n  return\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\n",
